  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nFog... Onshore breeze off the Bay has kept patchy fog around \nportions of east-central into far northeast Wisconsin through \nearly this afternoon, though dense fog has lifted. Signal then \nonce again exists for fog development late tonight into Friday \nmorning due to continued onshore flow and light winds aloft, \nthough remain uncertain if this will materialize as fog or low \nstratus. CAMs remain divided in this regard, though current \nthinking is that there may be just enough mixing to keep low \nstratus deck elevated. Radiational cooling late tonight is also \nnot looking as good as it did this morning. For the above reasons,\nhave opted out of issuing an advisory, though trends will have to\nbe monitored into this evening.\n\nRain chances... Light rain chances increase Saturday evening as \nlow pressure ejects into northern Canada and trailing cold front \nprogresses over the upper Midwest. Moisture return along the slow-\nmoving boundary will nudge PWATs into the 1 to 1.25\" range \nSaturday into Sunday (~90th percentile), which should work in \ntandem with upper jet energy to keep periodic rain chances in the \nforecast through the weekend. Blended PoPs continue to trend in \nthe 20 to 40% range, though suspect these will have to be \nincreased within the next day or two with front in the vicinity. \nNBM probabilities show a 20 to 40% chance for receiving 0.25\" of \nQPF through Monday. Beyond Monday, similar pattern of on/off rain \nchances exists as upper flow flattens out, opening the door for \nseveral shortwave passages within the 500 mb wave train. However, \noverall rainfall amounts should be held to a minimum with high \npressure in the area and lack of forcing mechanisms at the \nsurface.\n\nTemperatures... Arctic high sliding south in the wake of the front\nMonday into Tuesday will bring about a spell of below average\ntemperatures during the middle of next week, with highs only \ngetting into the mid 40s to mid 50s. However, cooler temperatures \nwill be short-lived as return flow sets up on Wednesday, warming\nhighs back up into the 50s and 60s. Monday night lows will fall\ninto the low to mid 30s across the north, though little to impacts\nare expected since the growing season has come to an end.",
